                                  RTPIP

     RTPIP is an OS/8 program which allows one to transfer files in either
direction to (or from) a single- or double-density RT-11 floppy disk.  The
usual sort of 'PIP' options are available: listing the directory, deleting
files or 'zeroing' a new disk.  Wildcards are not allowed, hence only one
file at a time may be moved, but unlike OS/8 PIP, the creation date of the
file is not changed.  (To facilitate moving many files at once, RTPIP may
be called from a BATCH file.)  OS/8 input files must be on a directory de-
vice (i.e. disks or DECtapes), however non-file-structured output devices
such as the TTY: are allowed.

     RTPIP prints a '*' after it is loaded, then waits for a command line
to be entered.  In order to process RT-11 filenames correctly, the command
line is not read by the OS/8 command decoder, but rather by a similar rou-
tine built into RTPIP.  The command line has the general form:

                    *output spec < input spec /options

where:  'output spec' may be either DEVICE:FILENAME.EX for OS/8, or
                                    FILENAME.EXT       for RT-11

and,    'input spec' is similar.  Note especially that NO RT-11 de-
         vice specification is allowed!  'DSK:' is assumed for OS/8.

        'options' are limited to: /D,/L,/R,/W,/V,/Z,/0,/1.  Only a
         single option is generally recognized, with the exception
         of the /0 and /1 options.  These two options only need to
         be specified once, rather than on each line.  The meaning
         of each option is as follows:

        /0 - the RT-11 floppy is in Drive 0
        /1 - the RT-11 floppy is in Drive 1 (this is the default)
        /D - Delete the RT-11 file specified
        /I - Image mode transfers (with /R or /W - see below)
        /L - List the RT-11 directory (default output dev is TTY:)
        /R - Read the RT-11 file
        /W - Write the RT-11 file
        /Z - Zero the RT-11 disk (asks 'Are you sure?' - ans. Y,N)
        /V - print the program version number

     The existence of the /R and /W options may appear puzzling since the
syntax of the command line itself would seem to show which file is being
read and which is being written.  But a moment's reflection will indicate
that without a device specification for the RT-11 disk, some other method
is necessary to resolve command lines such as: FILE.EXT<FILE.EXT.  Which
file is on the OS/8 device DSK:, and which is on the RT-11 disk?

     Since the option switches always refer to the RT-11 file, adding '/R'
indicates that the input comes from the RT-11 disk, while adding '/W' in-
dicates that a RT-11 file is being created.  If you forget to specify one
of the options, RTPIP will print an error message and then retype the com-
mand line so you can edit it.  Note that /D simply deletes the first file-
name specified, while /L and /Z completely ignore filename specifications.
(To send the directory listing to an OS/8 file, you must specify a DEVICE
name as well as a file name, e.g. DSK:RT11.DIR</L.)
     RTPIP is primarily intended for moving ASCII data files between OS/8
and RT-11 systems, hence the normal transfer mode includes an EOF check to
ensure that OS/8 files end with CTRL/Z, while RT-11 files end with a null.
Characters are also converted from 7-bit (RT-11) to 8-bit (OS/8), in keep-
ing with the standard convention in each system.  In order to allow an oc-
casional non-ASCII file to be moved, RTPIP does have a '/I' switch which
provides 'image-mode' transfers.  Note, however, that the FILESIZE may not
be preserved during a 'round-trip', even though using the /I option will
preserve the contents!  This anomoly is due to the fact that RT-11 files
contain 512 bytes/block, while OS/8 files have only 384.  OS/8 files whose
size is not a multiple of 4, or RT-11 files whose size is not a multiple
of 3, may 'grow' by 1 block when moved back and forth!

     When using RTPIP to transfer data files on a regular basis between
(say) an OS/8 data collection system and a RT-11 data analysis system, it
is advantageous to prepare a small BATCH file to control the operations.
RTPIP automatically uses the BATCH input and output devices in place of
the terminal when it is called in this manner, hence one has only to con-
struct a control file which mimicks the normal keyboard input:

                $JOB TO RUN RTPIP UNDER BATCH
                /HIT RETURN WHEN READY TO PROCEED
                $MSG: MOUNT RT-11 DISK IN DRIVE 1
                .R RTPIP
                */L$
                /RIGHT DISK? USE CTRL/C TO EXIT
                $MSG: IS THIS THE CORRECT DISK?
                .R RTPIP
                *FILE1.DAT<FILE1.DA/W
                *FILE2.DAT<FILE2.DA/W
                ...
                *FILEN.DAT<FILEN.DA/W$
                $END

     Note the two command lines above which end with a 'Dollar Sign' ($).
These cause the program to return to the OS/8 (or Batch) monitor so that
the next job-step can be performed.  The first exit occurs after listing
the directory, the second after copying the last file.  Such Batch Input
files can easily be created by the data collection program for use at the
end of the day, or even from a directory listing, if you are a TECO guru!


     Use of RTPIP in conjunction with the D0,D1 floppy disk handlers:

     RTPIP is a very useful program for exchanging files between 8's and
11's since it allows one to access files with the normal RT-11 directory.
However, it has the limitation that the only access to such files is via
the program, hence the only operation which can be performed is to move
files one-at-a-time from one disk to another.  One cannot directly com-
pare two files (using OCOMP, for instance) to see if they are identical.

     In contrast, use of the D0/D1 handler allows -any- OS/8 program to
access data on a single-density RT-11 disk.  However, such access is via
the OS/8 directory (or else directly by block number), rather than via
the RT-11 directory.  Fortunately the two directory areas do not overlap,
so it is possible to 'zero' a disk with both OS/8 PIP and RTPIP and thus
keep track of files in both directories simultaneously.  To attempt such
foolishness, you should place a 'dummy' 1-block file at the beginning of
the RT-11 directory, and a similar 13-block(10) file at the beginning of
the OS/8 directory.  (Use a command such as '.PUNCH D1:DUMMY</I=15'.)
OS/8 file sizes should be a multiple of 4, RT-11 files a multiple of 3.


                      Using RTPIP for archival storage

     Since RTPIP can operate with double-density disks as well as single-
density, it is possible to use this program to store files totaling up to
1298 OS/8 blocks on a single disk - a 32% improvement over the 981 blocks
available via the normal OS/8 double-density handler.  The extra storage
capacity comes from the use of 'byte-mode' rather than 'word-mode'.  Note
that the D0/D1 handler also uses byte-mode to get nearly 40% more storage
on a single-density disk.  However it is not possible to use this handler
with double-density disks due to a hardware limitation.  (It CAN be used
with single-density disks on a RX02 drive, however.)  Thus to gain access
to all the disk space available, it is necessary to use the RTPIP program.

     The major disadvantage to storing OS/8 files in this way is that ac-
cess to them is somewhat restricted, as discussed above.  For archiving
large data sets which are needed only infrequently, however, this method
is quite viable.  Such files are less transportable than those stored on
single-density disks, but they can be read on RX02-based PDP-11 systems.


                            RTPIP Error Messages

Bad slash option        Option was not /0,/1,/D,/I,/L,/R,/V,/W or /Z

No option selected      Self-explanatory command line error

Bad directory           A valid RT-11 directory was not found

No output file          Self-explanatory command line error

Handler fetch failed    The OS/8 handler specified could not be loaded

OS/8 I/O error          The OS/8 handler reported a fatal error condition

Cannot spec. RT-11 dev  Only OS/8 device specifications are allowed

Need file name          An OS/8 file-structured device was specified

Out dev full            Not enough room for the RT-11 directory listing

File not found          Either the OS/8 file or the RT-11 file was missing

Segment full - SQUEEZE  The RT-11 directory is full (try to SQUEEZE it)

No room on floppy       The RT-11 floppy cannot contain the new file

Floppy I/O error        The RT-11 handler reported a fatal error condition

OS/8 File overflow      The RT-11 file is too big to fit on the OS/8 device

Non-file input not...   Input from non-file devices (TTY:) is not allowed
